{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Vocabulary — {{ project.title }}{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

<h1>Vocabulary</h1>
<p class="muted">
  Every term this project's schema resolves to, in one place: item types,
  link verbs, field sets, and the keys the engine owns. Each entry gives
  its definition, its scope, where it points and what points at it, its
  fields, and a worked example of how it is written — hand-written for the
  bundled standard's terms, generated from the resolved schema for every
  other. It is generated from the resolved schema — base standard,
  presets, and this project's own overlay, in that order — so a term that
  exists here exists in the engine, and a preset the build does not enable
  is not here. A term with no definition says so: project terms are not
  required to define themselves.
</p>
<p class="muted">
  Above the index, the coverage spine: the verbs that close coverage --
  <em>addresses</em>, <em>satisfies</em>, <em>verifies</em> -- drawn as
  arrows reading subject verb object, each label carrying the status gate
  the build enforces, such as <code>satisfies [accepted]</code>.
  Each item type carries a small diagram of its own connections beside its
  heading. An arrow reads <em>subject verb object</em>: a box on the left
  means that type's own front matter names this one, and a box on the right
  means this type's front matter names it. Verbs that connect the same pair
  in the same direction share one arrow and stack their labels on it.
</p>

{{ vocabulary_html | safe }}

{% endblock %}
